    "updated_at": "2023-09-10T14:41:06Z",
    "body": "This is tricky to fix completely, because of how the HTML Standard somewhat glosses over parallelism and multi-threaded/multi-process data structures.\r\n\r\nIn theory, data structures like the frame tree should be maintained in a centralized \"browser process\", with all modifications and consultations happening there. The closest we have to that is the [session history traversal parallel queue](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#centralized-modifications-of-session-history). But we don't maintain the frame tree there; instead we have a variety of pointers like [content navigable](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#content-navigable), [parent](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#nav-parent), and [document's browsing context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-bc).\r\n\r\nWe don't have that sort of discipline in the spec, and it would be pretty hard to move there. For one thing, we'd need to split various objects (especially `Document`) into their normal JS-object representations, and their canonical \"browser process representatives\" which hold this centralized data. We'd also need to be much more careful about avoiding the generic \"in parallel\". Right now, in theory while \"in parallel\", anything goes and the frame tree could be modified between any step. So it's not just that we'd want to add a check as the first substep of navigate step 20, but we'd also check in between literally every in-parallel step...\r\n\r\nThat said, we can adopt the suggestions you give, as it sounds like they might be the more serious cases.\r\n\r\nI guess the predicate to check would be, is _navigable_'s active browsing context non-null. Right? Because _navigable_ itself is still around, captured by the spec's \"closure\". And there's no actual \"destroyed\" predicate on navigables.",
